---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const jsonSchema = `{
  "$schema": "./schema.json",
  "name": "example"
}`;
const yamlDirective = `# yaml-language-server: $schema=./schema.json
name: example`;
const yamlRoot = `$schema: ./schema.json
name: example`;
const tomlDirective = `#:schema ./schema.json
name = "example"`;
const tomlRoot = `"$schema" = "./schema.json"
name = "example"`;
const association = `[[schemas.associations]]
file = "settings/*.toml"
schema = "./schemas/settings.schema.json"`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Schema declarations" current="/guides/schema-declarations/">
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
      <h1>Schema declarations</h1>
      <p class="lead">Every file can declare its own schema — that's where the name comes from. For files that can't (or shouldn't) carry a <code>$schema</code>, config-level associations have you covered.</p>
    </header>

    <h2>JSON</h2>
    <pre><code>{jsonSchema}</code></pre>
    <p>JSON uses the conventional root <code>$schema</code> property.</p>

    <h2>YAML</h2>
    <pre><code>{yamlDirective}</code></pre>
    <pre><code>{yamlRoot}</code></pre>
    <p>The comment directive follows the YAML Language Server convention. dollarlint scans the first 25 lines for it before looking for a root <code>$schema</code> key.</p>

    <h2>TOML</h2>
    <pre><code>{tomlDirective}</code></pre>
    <pre><code>{tomlRoot}</code></pre>
    <p>The comment directive follows the Taplo and Even Better TOML convention. A root <code>$schema</code> key also works, but it must be quoted because <code>$</code> is not a bare TOML key character.</p>

    <h2>Config associations</h2>
    <p>When you can't — or don't want to — add a declaration to each file, associations let you cover them from your config instead.</p>
    <pre><code>{association}</code></pre>
    <p>Association globs are matched against each discovered file's path relative to the validation root.</p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>Precedence order: in-file declaration first, then config association, then catalog match. If none of those hit, the file gets skipped.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
